<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gordian III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">225–244</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gordian III was Roman emperor from 238 to 244, raised to the throne at thirteen after the deaths of his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordian-i">Gordian I</a>, his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordian-ii">Gordian II</a> and the senatorial emperors Pupienus and Balbinus. Guided by his praetorian prefect Timesitheus, he disbanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-iii-augusta">Third Legion Augusta</a> for its part in the Gordians' fall and campaigned against the Sasanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in 244, perhaps killed by his own troops.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gordian-iii.jpg" alt="Gordian III" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_Gordianus_III_Louvre_Ma1063.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gordian III was born on 20 January 225, the son of Maecia Faustina, daughter of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordian-i">Gordian I</a> and sister of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordian-ii">Gordian II</a>, and her husband Junius Balbus, who died before 238. Their names come from the unreliable Historia Augusta, and very little is known of his early life before his acclamation. In 238 a rebellion in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> proclaimed his grandfather and uncle joint emperors against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximinus-thrax">Maximinus Thrax</a>, but it was suppressed within a month by Cappellianus, the governor of Numidia and a loyal supporter of Maximinus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Senate, hostile to Maximinus, elected Pupienus and Balbinus as joint emperors. As these senators were not popular, it raised Gordian to the rank of Caesar. Maximinus' march met difficulties in an Alpine winter, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>, short of supplies, he besieged the city; after four weeks his demoralised army mutinied and the Legio II Parthica murdered him. Pupienus and Balbinus then faced popular riots, military discontent and an enormous fire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praetorian-guard">Praetorian Guard</a> killed them. Gordian was proclaimed sole emperor, probably around August, at the age of 13. Because of his youth, the imperial government passed to the aristocratic families who controlled Rome through the Senate.</p><p>In 240 Sabinianus revolted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> but was quickly defeated. In 241 Gordian married Furia Sabinia Tranquillina, daughter of the newly appointed praetorian prefect Timesitheus, who as father-in-law and chief of the Guard became the de facto ruler of the empire. Severe earthquakes in his reign led him to consult the Sibylline Books.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, Gordian opened the doors of the Temple of Janus for the last time in Roman history and sent a large army east. The Sasanians were driven back over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and defeated at the Battle of Resaena in 243. Timesitheus then died in unclear circumstances, and Gaius Julius Priscus and later his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-arab">Marcus Julius Philippus</a>, Philip the Arab, became the Praetorian Prefects. Around February 244 the Sasanians fought hard to halt the Roman advance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>.</p><p>The accounts of Gordian's fate differ. An inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> claims a major Sasanian victory near modern Fallujah and Gordian's death. Roman sources say the soldiers proclaimed Philip emperor, that he made a shameful peace, and that Gordian died as the army left; Zonaras says he fell from his horse in battle. One view holds that he was murdered by his frustrated army at Zaitha, and scholars regard the Sasanian version as better than the Roman one. Accounts of his burial also differ: David S. Potter says Philip brought the body to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and arranged his deification, while Edwell, Dodgeon and Lieu say it was buried at Zaitha.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gordian III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gordian III</b> later disbanded the legion in punishment, though it was restored in 253.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Circesium, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=237" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gordian III"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=237">What was here in 237 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
